From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 22:02:35 +0000
Subject: net: lantiq: xrx500: count packets and bytes in per-CPU stats

The four packet and byte counters lived in the per-port private struct
under one struct u64_stats_sync, and two unrelated contexts wrote it: the
CBM receive bottom half from softirq, and ndo_start_xmit under the port's
tx_lock. On 32-bit MIPS u64_stats_sync is a bare seqcount with no lock of
its own -- u64_stats_update_begin() only disables preemption there -- so
it requires the caller to serialise its writers, and nothing did. The
receive side's irqsave variant excluded that CPU's own interrupts and
nothing else.

That was survivable only while receive ran on one CPU. It no longer does:
the CBM interrupt lines are pinned one per CPU, so a receive bottom half
on one CPU and a transmit on another now write the same seqcount at the
same time. Two concurrent writers can leave the sequence odd, and an odd
sequence is a reader inside u64_stats_fetch_retry() that never gets a
consistent sample -- `ip -s link` spinning, not merely miscounting. The
counts are wrong long before that: an interleaved pair of updates loses
increments outright.

Move the four counters to the netdev core's per-CPU struct
pcpu_sw_netstats, which exists for exactly this. Setting
dev->pcpu_stat_type = NETDEV_PCPU_STAT_TSTATS before register_netdev()
has the core allocate dev->tstats and free it again on both the
registration-failure and the unregister path, so the driver has nothing
to unwind. The writers become dev_sw_netstats_rx_add() and
dev_sw_netstats_tx_add(), each touching only the running CPU's own
seqcount, and ndo_get_stats64 sums across CPUs with
dev_fetch_sw_netstats(). Every seqcount then has exactly one writer by
construction, which is what makes the reader's retry loop terminate. Same
shape as am65-cpsw-nuss and r8169.

The two drop counters stay where they are. They are atomic_long_t, so
they never had a writer-side seqcount to corrupt, and ndo_get_stats64
goes on reading them outside the aggregation so a drop cannot force a
re-read of the packet and byte pairs. struct intel_xrx500_port_stats is
now just those two.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/include/intel_xrx500.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/include/intel_xrx500.h
index a4dd49bdfdf2..d4c66822882b 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/include/intel_xrx500.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/include/intel_xrx500.h
@@ -14,7 +14,6 @@
 #include <linux/phylink.h>
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
-#include <linux/u64_stats_sync.h>
 
 /*
  * Forward declarations.
@@ -53,39 +52,30 @@ struct dma_tx_desc;
 #define INTEL_XRX500_DP_PORT_WAN  15
 
 /**
- * struct intel_xrx500_port_stats - per-port traffic counters.
- *
- * @rx_packets: frames received and delivered to the stack.
- *
- * @rx_bytes:   bytes received (post-CRC strip).
- *
- * @tx_packets: frames successfully posted to the egress DMA.
- *
- * @tx_bytes:   bytes posted to egress.
- *
+ * struct intel_xrx500_port_stats - per-port drop counters.
  * @rx_dropped: receive-path drops (allocation failure, queue full, ...).
- *
  * @tx_dropped: transmit-path drops.
  *
- * @syncp:      seqcount-based torn-read protection for the u64_stats_t
- *              fields above.
- *
- * The two drop counters use atomic_long_t because they (a) are incremented
- * from drop fastpaths that have no obligation to advance under the same
- * seqcount sequence as the packet/byte pair, and (b) atomic_long_t avoids
- * forcing the caller to acquire u64_stats_update_begin in the drop-only path.
- * On 64-bit architectures (BITS_PER_LONG == 64) the u64_stats_sync discipline
- * collapses to a no-op; on 32-bit architectures u64_stats_init must be called
- * before any update.
+ * The packet and byte counters are NOT here. They live in the netdev core's
+ * per-CPU &struct pcpu_sw_netstats, requested by setting
+ * dev->pcpu_stat_type = %NETDEV_PCPU_STAT_TSTATS before register_netdev();
+ * the writers use dev_sw_netstats_rx_add() / dev_sw_netstats_tx_add() and
+ * ndo_get_stats64 sums them with dev_fetch_sw_netstats(). That is what puts
+ * exactly one writer on each seqcount -- the CPU that owns it -- which a
+ * single per-port u64_stats_sync could not do once the receive bottom half
+ * and ndo_start_xmit ran on different CPUs.
+ *
+ * The two drop counters stay atomic_long_t: they are bumped from drop
+ * fastpaths with no obligation to advance in step with a packet/byte pair,
+ * and an atomic needs no writer-side seqcount at all, so it is immune to the
+ * same hazard. ndo_get_stats64 reads them outside the aggregation.
+ *
+ * Zero-initialised by alloc_etherdev_mq(), which backs the per-port struct
+ * via netdev_priv() -- ATOMIC_LONG_INIT(0) is a zero bit pattern.
  */
 struct intel_xrx500_port_stats {
-	u64_stats_t		rx_packets;
-	u64_stats_t		rx_bytes;
-	u64_stats_t		tx_packets;
-	u64_stats_t		tx_bytes;
 	atomic_long_t		rx_dropped;
 	atomic_long_t		tx_dropped;
-	struct u64_stats_sync	syncp;
 };
 
 /**
